| 1 | 3 | Getting to books. *What is the main idea, and how do the parts hold it up?* | 21 | g3-la-inform; g3-si-inform | WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform | ELA grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39), Reading against the odds: its informational set on how books reach children (the paired articles on book carriers today and the 1930s packhorse librarians), informative writing and main idea (e3-informative, e3-main-idea), class discussion (e3-discussion). ELD days 1 to 21, weeks 1 to 5. Inform | LA Inform in both modes (owner year; the K to 2 band's grade 2 began these ids): students take apart one section of the ELA unit's packhorse librarian article (how its heading groups facts under one main idea; "for example" and "also" adding a detail), map the main idea and details on the text map, then build an informative piece in grouped parts with headings, linking words and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The ids' parts, in our words: find what the text is mainly about and the details that hold it up; see how headings and grouping organize it; tell or write about a topic with a clear opening, grouped facts, linking words and a closing. SI Inform (owner year): share facts about the book carriers in a short sharing time, then ask and answer a clarifying question ("What do you mean by ___? Can you say more about ___?"). Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's paragraph with a topic sentence. Forms: text, an informative piece in grouped parts; sentence, a topic sentence and a detail joined with "for example"; word, main idea, detail, heading, grouped, and the topic words of the article. Model: the text map, 2 to 3 stage (grouped boxes with headings). The "tell, ask, retell" routine is taught in days 1 to 3 and kept all year. Shared task at three entry points: share what the class learned about one way books reach children and write a grouped piece about it (1 and 2 with a labeled diagram, the text map, the word bank and the cloze "The main idea is ___. One detail is ___."; 3 and 4 with heading frames and "For example, ___. Also, ___."; 5 and 6 with the text map only, adding a conclusion of their own). Change of audience: the sharing time told to a partner from the class, then written as a page for a grade 2 reader. Talk about language: what a heading does for a reader. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new short informational page on how a mobile library reaches a town (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and name its main idea and two details; share it with the teacher and answer one clarifying question; write a grouped piece with a heading; then a respond day. Out: the full research piece and the reading lessons (ELA). Tools: a model-sentence replay tool for the topic sentence (paper path: the teacher reads it). Curio Class writes the word cards, the frames per level, the model page and the new page | First because ELA's informational set runs weeks 1 to 8 and informing is the use the class needs for every later host. Grade 3 owns these ids, so the year opens on the one most English learners met last in grade 2's first unit. The talk routine and the 2 to 3 text map start here | Candidate: California 1.26, all four domains in the unit; PRIME Match.LE, one interpretive item on the new page; bar item 12, the self-check page's first use | Children kept from books by distance or poverty are the host's theme; no student is asked how many books they own or whether there is a library near home. Home language: a sharing turn may start in the home language; detalle and detail, idea and idea are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity; spanish (family note) |
| 2 | 3 | Who decides in our town. *What does each level of government do, and why?* | 17 | g3-ss-explain | WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Expressive | Social studies grade 3 unit 2 (days 25 to 48), Who decides here: levels of government (ss3-levels-of-government); how the land changed (ss3-land-changes, social studies grade 3 unit 1, days 1 to 24) just studied and retrieved. ELD days 22 to 38, weeks 5 to 8. Explain; narrate (SI review) | SS Explain in both modes (owner year): students hear and read the social studies unit's page on who makes which rules (town, state, nation) and take apart one sentence that says what a level decides and why ("The town council decides ___, so ___"); explain what one level of government does and why, then how the town's land changed and what that changed for the people, with the teacher first and then alone, in a short paragraph. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's resource-to-use chain. Forms: text, an explanation of a decision and its effect; sentence, cause with "so" and "because" and a present tense for what a level does; word, government, level, decides, council, land, local. Model: the cause chain, 2 to 3 stage. SI Narrate (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate, owned in this year's unit 8): recount how the town's land changed, in order, from the unit 1 map walk ("At the beginning ___. In the middle ___. In the end ___."); look-for: three parts in order with a time word. Shared task at three entry points: explain what one level of government does and why, and how the land changed (1 and 2 with picture cards of each level and of the land then and now, the word bank and the cloze "The ___ government ___."; 3 and 4 with "so" and "because" frames; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, one change in the land and its effect in a short paragraph). Change of purpose: the explanation given to a partner, then written as a caption for the class's town map. If later: days 22 to 24 come before the host's government pages, so they work the land change from unit 1, already studied. End-of-unit assessment (two days): hear a new short page on who fixes a town's park (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and order the chain; explain it to the teacher; write a short explanation; then a respond day. Out: the civics content itself (social studies). Curio Class writes the level and land cards, the frames and the new page | Second because social studies teaches the levels of government in weeks 5 to 10, and explaining what a level does follows informing about a topic. Narrate comes back early as SI review so it is not met only at the year's end | Candidate: PRIME Depth.KLU, the cause chain's links named openly; California 1.23, a long-term English learner note on "so" and "because" in writing | No student is asked how government works in another country or whether a family votes or is a citizen. Home language: gobierno and government share a root but look different; local and local look the same. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 3 | 3 | The class deliberation. *What is our claim, and what does the source say?* | 19 | g3-ss-argue; g3-si-argue | WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue | Social studies grade 3 unit 2 (days 25 to 48), Who decides here: its class deliberation on a local question and its vote (ss3-deliberation); a claim with evidence (ss3-claim-evidence, social studies grade 3 unit 1, days 1 to 24) retrieved; opinion with a reason (e3-opinion), ELA grade 3 unit 3 (days 81 to 119), later and not used. ELD days 39 to 57, weeks 8 to 12. Argue | SS Argue in both modes (owner year): students read two short sources on the host's local question and take apart the sentence that gives the claim and the one that gives its evidence ("The source says ___"); make a claim with evidence and take part in the class deliberation, with the teacher first and then in groups. SI Argue (owner year): negotiate a group choice: state a view with reasons, ask "Why do you think ___?", build on another's reason, and agree on a decision ("We decided ___"; "I changed my mind because ___"). Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's claim from sources and unit 1 of social studies' claim with evidence. Forms: text, a claim with two reasons and their source; sentence, "I think ___ because ___" with "the source says"; word, claim, evidence, source, deliberate, instead, build on. Model: the claim chart, 2 to 3 stage. Shared task at three entry points: make a claim with evidence and help the group decide (1 and 2 with the question and its sources in pictures on the claim chart, the word bank and the cloze "I think ___. The source says ___."; 3 and 4 with "My claim is ___. The source says ___. I agree with ___ because ___."; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two sources first). Change of audience: the claim said to a group, then written as a note to the town council. Days 49 to 57 run just after the host's vote, from the class's own record of it. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read two new short sources on a made-up town question (where a crosswalk goes; Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say which claim each supports; reach a decision with a group while the teacher listens; write a claim with evidence; then a respond day. Out: the deliberation itself and the civics content (social studies), opinion writing (ELA) | Alongside social studies' deliberation, which needs exactly this talk; SI and SS Argue share it, so they sit in one unit. Before ELA's opinion unit, so ELA meets students who already state a view with reasons | Candidate: PRIME Breadth.LE, two argue ids met on one host; bar item 4, the deliberation's talk frames | The local question is one the host picks (a park rule, a crosswalk), never immigration, policing or anything that touches a family's status. "I changed my mind" is praised, never forced. Home language: evidencia and evidence, decidir and decide are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 4 | 3 | Life cycles and forces. *What happens first, next, and why?* | 19 | g3-sc-explain; g3-si-explain | WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain | Science grade 3 unit 3 (days 37 to 58; weeks 10 to 15), Growing up and taking after: life cycles (s3-life-cycles); balanced and unbalanced forces (s3-balanced-forces, science grade 3 unit 1, days 1 to 16; weeks 1 to 4) retrieved. ELD days 58 to 76, weeks 12 to 16. Explain | SC Explain in both modes (owner year): students read the science unit's life-cycle model and take apart its sequence sentences ("First it is a ___. Next it ___, so ___"); explain a life cycle from the model, then how a bigger push changes the swing's motion from the unit 1 force test, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Explain (owner year): explain how a group solved a problem (ordering the life-cycle cards, setting up the force test again from the record) and why they chose that way. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's material-and-use explanation and science unit 1's force test. Forms: text, an explanation in steps with a cause; sentence, sequence words with "so" and "when the push is bigger, ___"; word, life cycle, stage, force, push, balanced, unbalanced. Model: the cause chain, 2 to 3 stage. Shared task at three entry points: explain a life cycle and a force change, and how the group sorted it (1 and 2 with the life-cycle model or the force test to point to, the word bank and the cloze "First it is a ___. Next it ___."; 3 and 4 with "First ___. Next ___, so ___."; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, a pattern explained from two observations). Change of audience: the explanation given to the group, then to a grade 2 class as a short talk. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new short page on a plant's life cycle not studied (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and order the stages; explain it to the teacher, and how the group checked its order; write the explanation; then a respond day. Out: the investigation (science) | Alongside the science life-cycles unit in weeks 10 to 15. SI Explain shares the cause chain with the science row, so they sit in one unit, the first full explain unit of the year | Candidate: California 3.3, the language-or-learning note on sequence words; PRIME Match.KLU, explain taught as its own use | Living things only, no family trees or "who you take after" prompts about a student's own family. Home language: ciclo and cycle are cognates; fuerza and force share a root but look different, so the card carries both. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 5 | 3 | Arrays and equal groups. *How did you solve it, and why does each step work?* | 16 | g3-ma-explain | WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Expressive | Math grade 3 unit 4 (days 62 to 77), Division and the multiplication table: area and the distributive idea (m3-area-distributive), and math grade 3 unit 5 (days 78 to 91), Multiplying and dividing larger numbers (m3-mult-div-problems). ELD days 77 to 92, weeks 16 to 19. Explain; inform (SI review) | MA Explain in both modes (owner year): students read a worked solution from the math unit and take apart its step sentences ("There are ___ rows of ___, so ___"); explain how they solved a multiplication or division problem with an array or area model, saying why each step works, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Inform (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform, owned in unit 1): share the facts of a problem before solving it ("I learned that ___. For example, ___."), and ask a clarifying question of a partner's facts; look-for: one clarifying question asked and answered. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's explanation of a sum. Forms: text, a solution explained step by step; sentence, "There are ___ rows of ___, so ___" and "This works because ___"; word, array, equal groups, rows of, times, product, area. Model: the cause chain, 2 to 3 stage, as steps. Shared task at three entry points: explain how a problem was solved (1 and 2 with the array or area model to point along, the word bank and the cloze; 3 and 4 with "First I ___. Next I ___, so ___. This works because ___."; 5 and 6 with the model only, why the strategy works for a new number). Change of audience: explained to a partner, then recorded as steps a classmate who was absent could follow. Tight at 16 days: the respond day carries the review forms. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new worked solution with one step missing (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say which step is missing; explain a new problem to the teacher; write the steps; then a respond day. Out: the math teaching (math) | Alongside the two math units in weeks 13 to 19; before the mid-year check, so explain is seen on math content before the check samples it | Candidate: PRIME Depth.LE, why each step works, not only what it is; California 5.27, why "so" is hard after a number sentence, with a remedy | Word problems use school and market settings with no prices that rank families. Home language: multiplicaciA3n and multiplication, divisiA3n and division are cognates; the teacher names that some countries write the division sign differently. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 6 | 3 | Surviving where you live. *What is our claim, and what does the data show?* | 18 | g3-sc-argue | WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Expressive | Science grade 3 unit 4 (days 59 to 78; weeks 15 to 20), Surviving where you live (s3-habitat-survival), just studied; traits and the environment (s3-environment-traits, science grade 3 unit 3, days 37 to 58; weeks 10 to 15) retrieved; science grade 3 unit 5 (days 82 to 97; weeks 21 to 25), Environments change, runs alongside and is not leaned on. ELD days 96 to 113, weeks 20 to 23. Argue; explain (SI review) | SC Argue in both modes (owner year): students read the science unit's habitat data and take apart a claim sentence and its evidence ("The data shows ___"); argue whether an organism survives well in a habitat and whether the environment affects a trait, with two pieces of evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Explain (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain, owned in unit 4): explain how the group chose which evidence to use and why; look-for: "we chose ___ because ___". Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's claim from a material test and unit 4's life cycles. Forms: text, a claim with two pieces of evidence; sentence, "Our claim is ___. The data shows ___. Another clue is ___."; word, claim, evidence, habitat, survive, trait. Model: the claim chart, 2 to 3 stage. Shared task at three entry points: argue a survival claim from the unit's evidence (1 and 2 with the evidence in pictures on the claim chart, the word bank and the cloze "I think ___ survives because ___."; 3 and 4 with the claim frames; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a different claim with a second piece of evidence). Change of purpose: the claim argued in a group, then written as a card for a zoo or park habitat sign. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read new data on an animal in a habitat not studied (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say which claim it supports; argue it to the teacher; write the claim with two pieces of evidence; then a respond day. Out: the investigation (science) | Just after the science habitat unit, the nearest fit after the mid-year check; argue returns on science content after social studies' deliberation | Candidate: PRIME Breadth.ELD, argue on a second content area; bar item 13, argue returns harder (data, not a source) | Organisms that do not survive are talked about plainly and gently; no human migration is compared to animal habitats. Home language: hA�bitat and habitat are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 7 | 3 | Fractions on the line. *Which fraction is greater, and how do you know?* | 19 | g3-ma-argue | WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Expressive | Math grade 3 unit 6 (days 92 to 114), Fractions as numbers: comparing and equivalent fractions (m3-compare-fractions, m3-equivalent-fractions), just studied; math grade 3 unit 7 (days 117 to 133), its line plots with fractions, alongside. ELD days 114 to 132, weeks 23 to 27. Argue (SI review) | MA Argue in both modes (owner year): students read two classmates' claims about a pair of fractions and take apart the claim and its justification ("___ is greater than ___. The number line shows ___"); claim which fraction is greater or that two are equal, justify it with a number line or strips, and critique a classmate's claim, with the teacher first and then alone. The ids' parts, in our words: follow a claim and the reasoning that holds it up; say whether the reasoning works; make a claim, give a justification from a model, and answer another's claim with "I agree" or "I see it differently". SI Argue (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue, owned in unit 3): the group agrees which of two solutions is stronger; look-for: building on another's reason. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's number claims with a drawing and math unit 6's number line. Forms: text, a claim with a justification and a critique; sentence, comparatives with "than" and "I claim ___ because the model shows ___"; word, greater than, less than, equal to, whole, numerator, denominator. Model: the claim chart, 2 to 3 stage. Shared task at three entry points: claim and justify a fraction comparison, then critique (1 and 2 with fraction strips, the number line, the word bank and the cloze; 3 and 4 with the claim and agree frames; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a classmate's claim with their own model). Change of audience: argued with a partner, then written as a reply to a made-up classmate who got it wrong. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read two new claims about a new fraction pair (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say which the model supports; argue to the teacher; write a claim with its justification and a critique; then a respond day. Out: the math teaching (math) | Just after math's fractions unit, while its line plots use the same fractions; the last argue of the year before the year-end check | Candidate: PRIME Depth.LE, critique taught as its own move; California 4.1, the grade 2 drawing frame withdrawn | A critique is of the reasoning, never the classmate; made-up classmates only in written critiques. Home language: fracciA3n and fraction are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 8 | 3 | A story with a voice. *How does the character answer the problem?* | 25 | g3-la-narrate; g3-si-narrate | WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate | ELA grade 3 unit 4 (days 120 to 156), Water around the world: its read-aloud story of a girl whose village gets a new well; narrative writing (e3-narrative, ELA grade 3 unit 2, days 40 to 77, its pourquoi tales) and story parts (e3-story-parts, ELA grade 3 unit 3, days 81 to 119, the chapter-book series) retrieved; telling and reporting aloud (e3-speaking-reports, ELA grade 3 unit 3). ELD days 136 to 160, weeks 28 to 32. Narrate | LA Narrate in both modes (owner year): students take apart one scene of the well story (how dialogue and said-words show how the girl answers the problem; "at last" moving to the ending), map the problem, events and solution on the story map, then build their own story with dialogue and a clear ending, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Narrate (owner year): tell an event from school life with a beginning, middle and end and a feeling; the listener asks a question, and a third student retells it back. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's fable map and the chapter book's story parts. Forms: text, a story with a problem, events, a solution and dialogue; sentence, quoted speech with "said" and "asked", past tense; word, dialogue, problem, feelings, at last, finally. Model: the story map, 2 to 3 stage, finished. Shared task at three entry points: map the well story and write a story with dialogue and an ending (1 and 2 with the story map and picture cards, the word bank and the cloze "___ said, '___.' In the end ___."; 3 and 4 with dialogue frames and "At last, ___."; 5 and 6 with the story map only, adding a feeling shown through dialogue). Change of audience: the school event told to a partner who was there, then to a visitor who was not. End-of-unit assessment (two days): hear and read a new short story (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and map it; tell a school event to the teacher and retell the teacher's; write a story with dialogue; then a respond day. The end-of-unit record and the year's sheet go on to grade 4's teacher. Out: the reading and writing lessons (ELA) | Last because ELA's water unit and its read-aloud story run weeks 24 to 32, and narrate is the use that most needs the year's talk routine in place. No course-level check samples it (Course-level checks, Weeks) | Candidate: PRIME Match.LE, the story's structure named; bar item 10, the change of audience | Own-story prompts follow APPROACH "Sensitive ground": an invented character or a school event is always allowed; no prompt asks about a journey to this country. The well story's village is named and its people are not pitied. A story may be planned in the home language. Home language: nervioso and nervous, furioso and furious are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity; spanish |
| 9 | 4 | The same amount. *Are these fractions equal, and can you convince the group?* | 17 | g4-ma-argue; g4-si-argue | WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue | Math grade 4 unit 2 (days 9 to 25), Fraction equivalence and comparison (m4-equivalent-fractions, m4-compare-fractions); math grade 4 unit 1 (days 1 to 8), factors and multiples, runs first; class discussion (e4-discussion), ELA grade 4 unit 1 (days 1 to 38), alongside. ELD days 1 to 17, weeks 1 to 4. Argue | MA Argue in both modes (WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive and WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Expressive, carried; owned in grade 5): students read a classmate's justification and take apart its claim, its reason and its "therefore"; justify that two fractions are equivalent, or compare them, with strips or a number line, and respond to a classmate's reasoning, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Argue (owner year for WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue): persuade the group to one way of showing the equivalence, disagree politely with a reason ("I see it differently because ___"), and reach a compromise recorded on the claim chart. Retrieval on day 2: grade 3's fraction claims on a number line (unit 7). Forms: text, a justification with a claim, a reason and a conclusion; sentence, "I claim ___. One reason is ___. Therefore, ___."; word, equivalent, justify, the model shows, therefore, compromise. Model: the claim chart, 4 to 5 stage begun. The "plan, check, report" routine is taught in days 1 to 3 on a factor puzzle and kept all year. Shared task at three entry points: justify an equivalence and reach the group's choice (1 and 2 with fraction strips and the claim chart, the word bank and the cloze "___ is equivalent to ___. The model shows ___."; 3 and 4 with the claim and "I see it differently" frames; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, saying which of two solutions is stronger). Change of audience: argued in the group, then written for a grade 3 student who thinks the bigger denominator means the bigger fraction. If later: days 1 to 8 come before math's fractions unit, so they retrieve grade 3's fraction claims and teach the routine and the compromise talk on the factor puzzle. Tight at 17 days with a new routine: the respond day carries review forms. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read two new justifications for a new fraction pair (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say which holds; argue to the teacher; write a justification and a reply; then a respond day. Out: the math teaching (math) | First because math's fractions unit runs weeks 2 to 5 and argue is the use that sets up group talk; the year's routine moves talk from pairs to groups of four, so it starts on the use that needs disagreement | Candidate: California 1.20, a named group routine; PRIME Breadth.KLU, two argue ids on one host | Disagreeing politely is taught as a skill; no student is made to give way. Long-term English learners: "therefore" in writing is the look-for, since the social talk is already there. Home language: equivalente and equivalent, evidencia and evidence, compromiso and compromise are cognates (compromiso can also mean a promise, which the teacher names). Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 10 | 4 | Our neighbors. *What does the research show, and does a second source agree?* | 18 | g4-ss-argue | None | Social studies grade 4 unit 2 (days 17 to 38), Our neighbors: Canada and Mexico, its research (ss4-canada-mexico-research); primary sources (ss4-primary-source, social studies grade 4 unit 4, days 60 to 79) come later and are not used; the unit works from the research unit's own maps, photographs and records. ELD days 18 to 35, weeks 4 to 7. Argue; inform (SI review) | SS Argue in both modes (WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Interpretive and WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Expressive, carried; owned in grade 5): students read two short sources from the research unit on one question about Canada or Mexico and take apart the sentence that cites a source ("For example, the source says ___"); make a claim from research and check one source against another, citing both, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Inform (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform, owned in unit 11): give and follow directions for the group's research task, each member's role named; look-for: "Do you mean ___?" asked and answered. Retrieval on day 2: grade 3's claim from a source in the deliberation. Forms: text, a claim from two sources; sentence, "My claim is ___. For example, the source says ___. Therefore, ___."; word, source, research, claim, region, border, therefore. Model: the claim chart, 4 to 5 stage. Shared task at three entry points: claim from two sources (1 and 2 with the sources in pictures on the claim chart, the word bank and the cloze "The source shows ___. Another source ___."; 3 and 4 with the claim frames; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading of the source). Change of audience: told to the group, then written for the class's research wall. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read two new short sources on a question about the border's rivers (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and say whether they agree; argue to the teacher; write a claim citing both; then 