## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grade 3 mid-year (week 19) | 5 | 2 | 1 | Grade 3 days 93 and 94, respond day 95. Samples units 1 to 4 on new content: a read and heard informational page on a new topic (name its main idea and two details), a heard explanation of a new town decision (order the chain), two short sources on a new local question (which claim each supports) and a page on a new animal's life cycle (order the stages), then a talk task heard by the teacher (a sharing turn answered with a clarifying question; a group decision, or a recount in three parts, unit 2's narrate review) and a written grouped piece or explanation. Unit 5's end-of-unit record (days 90 and 91) is carried unscored, not re-collected. Look-fors per entry point; the sheet shows "seen" or "not yet" and feeds the respond day's groups and any move between entry points |
| Grade 3 year-end (week 27) | 7 | 2 | 1 | Grade 3 days 133 and 134, respond day 135. Samples units 4, 5 and 6 on new content: a new force test record (explain the change), a new worked multiplication solution (say why a step works), new habitat data (which claim it supports), then a talk task and a written claim with two pieces of evidence or an explanation. Unit 7's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. The year's sheet and the unit 8 record go on to grade 4's teacher |
| Grade 4 mid-year (week 15) | 12 | 2 | 1 | Grade 4 days 72 and 73, respond day 74. Samples units 9, 10 and 11 on new content: a new fraction justification (does it hold), two new short sources on a question about a neighbor country's land (do they agree), a new section on an animal defense (name its structure and defined term), then a talk task (a plan reported and checked; a compromise reached in a group of four while the teacher listens) and a written justification, claim or section. Unit 12's end-of-unit record is carried unscored |
| Grade 4 year-end (week 29) | 16 | 2 | 1 | Grade 4 days 143 and 144, respond day 145. Samples units 12, 13, 14 and 15 on new content: a new adventure scene (its theme and how it slows down), a new page on land changing (order the causes), a new short opinion (find its reasons), a new multi-digit solution (find the step), then a talk task (a recount for an absent classmate; a method explained) and a written opinion paragraph or explanation. Unit 16's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. The year's sheet and the unit 17 record go on to grade 5's teacher |
| Grade 5 mid-year (week 18) | 21 | 2 | 1 | Grade 5 days 86 and 87, respond day 88. Samples units 18, 19 and 20 on new content: a new two-scene story (how the scenes join), a new page and data on a plant (explain the energy, say what the data supports), two new paragraphs on one topic (name each structure), then a talk task (a recount across days; a progress report summed up by a classmate) and a written scene, claim or paragraph. Unit 21's end-of-unit record is carried unscored |
| Grade 5 year-end (week 28) | 24 | 2 | 1 | Grade 5 days 138 and 139, respond day 140. Samples units 21, 22 and 23 on new content: a new page on a made-up charter (explain how it shares power), a new decimal solution (why a step fits), a new short opinion (trace its reasons to its points), then a talk task (a process explained and a "why not" answered; a case made for a plan) and a written opinion paragraph or explanation. Unit 24's end-of-unit record is carried unscored. The year's sheet and the unit 25 record go on to grade 6's teacher |

**Weeks.** After-core checks do not take a core check week (docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md, "After-core
courses"). In the calendar the core frames give today (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md), the core course-level check weeks
are: grade 3 9, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 20, 21, 23, 24, 25, 28, 30, 31 and 32; grade 4 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 16, 19, 21, 22,
23, 27, 28, 30, 31 and 32; grade 5 10, 11, 12, 16, 17, 21, 22, 29, 30, 31 and 32. So the ELD checks sit in grade 3
weeks 19 and 27, grade 4 weeks 15 and 29, and grade 5 weeks 18 and 28: in each year, a free week near the middle that
follows a unit's end, and a late free week with one host-ordered unit left after it (grade 3's
weeks 26 and 27 are the only free weeks between core weeks 25 and 28; grade 4's 29 sits between core weeks 28 and 30;
grade 5's 28 is the last free week before core weeks 29 to 32). That is why each year's last unit (8, 17 and 25)
follows its year-end check: it is taught and assessed in full, and its end-of-unit record is passed on unscored.
The cost is stated plainly: no course-level check samples grade 3's LA Narrate ids and SI K-3 Narrate as its own row
(unit 8; SI Narrate is heard once, as unit 2's review use, in the mid-year talk task) or its MA Argue ids (unit 7,
carried unscored); grade 4's SC Argue and SS Explain rows (units 16 and 17; ids owned in grade 5, where the year's
SC and SS units are checked); grade 5's MA Argue and SS Argue ids (units 24 and 25). Each is checked by its
end-of-unit assessment, and that record and the teacher's sheet go on to the next teacher. CALENDAR.md rule 2
(one-to-one checks kept apart) is a K to 2 rule and does not bind here. `frame.py calendar` lists these six weeks beside
other after-core checks in the same weeks (For the lead, item 5); the weeks stay until the lead's calendar pass across
the after-core frames.

**How a check runs in 30 minutes.** A read or heard part (the teacher reads or plays the new text twice; students
also have it on paper) and the writing task go to the whole class at once; the talk task is heard one student or
one group at a time, about a minute a student, while the others work, over the two days. Each student is heard once
in a check. The respond day groups students by form and by entry point (at most four groups; the others rehearse in
pairs from the frame). No scores are stored; the teacher's paper sheet stays with the teacher (promise 2).

