{"strand": "ma", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-ma-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-ma-explain", "hands_on": "g4-ma-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "array; equal groups; rows of; times; so; because", "cross_subject": "math:m3-mult-div-problems (problems solved); math:m3-area-distributive (area model explained)", "notes": "Students explain how they solved multiplication and division problems in the math unit using arrays or area models, saying why each step works. Entry points (all explain how they solved a multiplication or division problem with an array or area model, saying why each step works): levels 1 and 2 with the array or area model to point along, a word bank and the cloze \"There are ___ rows of ___, so ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"First I ___. Next I ___, so ___. This works because ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining why the strategy works for a new number, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 base-ten cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a solution path from the problem to the answer; first, next and so, with present tense for how the model works; array, equal groups and rows of. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with \"so\". Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (multiplicación and multiplication, división and division). Owner year for MA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations of a solution path."}
{"strand": "ma", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-ma-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-ma-argue", "hands_on": "g4-ma-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "greater than; equal to; the number line shows; whole; claim; I agree with", "cross_subject": "math:m3-compare-fractions (comparing fractions); math:m3-equivalent-fractions (equivalent fractions)", "notes": "Students claim which fraction is greater or that two fractions are equal and justify it with a number line or model, then critique a classmate's claim. Entry points (all claim which fraction is greater or that two fractions are equal, justify it with a number line or model, and critique a classmate's claim): levels 1 and 2 with fraction strips and the number line, a word bank and the cloze \"___ is greater than ___. The number line shows ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"I claim ___ because the model shows ___. I agree with ___ because ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a classmate's claim with their own model, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 \"I know because\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim, its reason from a model and a critique; claim plus \"the number line shows\"; greater than, equal to and whole. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source (the model). Home language: a student may compare fractions in the home language first; fracción and fraction are cognates. Owner year for MA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims about math with reasons."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-sc-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-sc-explain", "hands_on": "g4-sc-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "life cycle; first; force; push; balanced; so", "cross_subject": "science:s3-life-cycles (host investigation); science:s3-balanced-forces (host investigation)", "notes": "Students explain life cycles and how balanced and unbalanced forces change motion, using models from the science unit. Entry points (all explain a life cycle and how balanced and unbalanced forces change motion, using the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the life-cycle model or the force test to point to, a word bank and the cloze \"First it is a ___. Next it ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"First ___. Next ___, so ___.\" and \"When the push is bigger, ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining a pattern from two observations, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 property cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation in stages or steps; first, next and so, with present tense for how things work; life cycle, force and balanced. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with \"so\". Home language: a student may name the stages in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (ciclo and cycle, fuerza and force share a root but look different, so the card carries both). Owner year for SC 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations with models."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-sc-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-sc-argue", "hands_on": "g4-sc-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "claim; the data shows; habitat; survive; trait; evidence", "cross_subject": "science:s3-habitat-survival (host investigation); science:s3-environment-traits (host investigation)", "notes": "Students argue whether some organisms survive well in a habitat and whether the environment affects traits, from evidence in the science unit. Entry points (all argue whether some organisms survive well in a habitat and whether the environment affects traits, from the unit's evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart in pictures, a word bank and the cloze \"I think ___ survives because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Our claim is ___. The data shows ___. Another clue is ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a different claim with a second piece of evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 \"the test showed\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with two pieces of evidence and an answer to another claim; claim plus \"the data shows\"; habitat, survive and trait. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; hábitat and habitat are cognates. Owner year for SC 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims with evidence."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-ss-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-ss-explain", "hands_on": "g4-ss-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "government; level; decides; so; because; land", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss3-levels-of-government (host unit); social-studies:ss3-land-changes (host unit)", "notes": "Students explain what each level of government does and how land changed over time in their region, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain what each level of government does and how land changed over time in the region): levels 1 and 2 with picture cards of each level and of the land then and now, a word bank and the cloze \"The ___ government ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 say what one level of government does and why, with \"so\" and \"because\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining one change in the land and its effect in a short paragraph, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 \"people use\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of how and why in a short paragraph; first, next and so, with present tense for what a government does; level, decides and land. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with \"so\". Home language: a student may talk through the levels in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (gobierno and government share a root but look different, and local and local look the same), and no student is asked how government works in another country. Owner year for SS 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations of how and why."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "3", "skill_id": "g3-ss-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g2-ss-argue", "hands_on": "g4-ss-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "claim; the source says; I agree with; evidence; deliberate; local", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss3-claim-evidence (host unit); social-studies:ss3-deliberation (host unit)", "notes": "Students make a claim with evidence and take part in a class deliberation on a local question, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all make a claim with evidence and take part in a class deliberation on a local question): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the question and its sources in pictures, a word bank and the cloze \"I think ___. The source says ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"My claim is ___. The source says ___. I agree with ___ because ___.\", off before the end-of-unit deliberation; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two sources before stating the claim, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 two-column picture chart is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from a source in a deliberation; claim plus \"the source says\" and \"I agree with ___ because\"; claim, evidence and deliberate. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source. Home language: a student may prepare the claim in the home language first; evidencia and evidence are cognates. Owner year for SS 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims from sources."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-si-narrate", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate", "assumes": "g3-si-narrate", "hands_on": "g5-si-narrate", "model_in": "story map", "model_out": "story map", "words": "after that; meanwhile; finally; our group; at first", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud)", "notes": "Students recount a group task they shared (a project, a field study) for a classmate who was absent, in order, with linking words, then listen to a recount and ask a follow-up. Entry points (all recount a group task for a classmate who was absent, in order, then ask a follow-up of another recount): levels 1 and 2 with the group's picture notes on the story map, a word bank of time words and the cloze \"At first we ___. After that ___. Finally ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"At first ___. Meanwhile ___. After that ___.\" and a follow-up card, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing what the absent classmate needs and leaving out the rest, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount for a listener who was not there, with an opening that says what the task was; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and the past progressive (\"we were measuring\"); after that, meanwhile and finally. Model: story map at the 4 to 5 stage, scenes and pacing (which moments get more time). Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language on the story map first; the absent classmate hears it in English. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-si-inform", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform", "assumes": "g3-si-inform", "hands_on": "g5-si-inform", "model_in": "text map", "model_out": "text map", "words": "the task is; step; plan; role; Do you mean; clarify", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud)", "notes": "Students give and follow directions for a group task, report the group's plan to the class and ask for clarification (\"Do you mean...?\"). Entry points (all give and follow directions for a group task, report the group's plan to the class and ask \"Do you mean ___?\"): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a picture text map, gestures, a word bank and the cloze \"Our plan is to ___. First ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Our plan is to ___. Each person will ___. Do you mean ___?\", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up another group's plan to check it, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report that gives the goal first and then the steps and roles; \"will\" for the plan and relative clauses for roles (\"the person who measures\"); in addition and for instance. Model: text map at the 4 to 5 stage, the structure of the plan (goal, steps, roles). Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language and report in English; plan and plan, rol and role are cognates. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-si-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain", "assumes": "g3-si-explain", "hands_on": "g5-si-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "because; as a result; so that; instead; the reason", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)", "notes": "Students explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan, using cause and sequence linking words. Entry points (all explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of the change, labeled step cards, a word bank and the cloze \"We ___. Then ___, so we changed to ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"At first we ___. As a result, ___. So we ___ instead.\" off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, giving the reason for the change in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation that moves from the first plan to the change and its reason; \"as a result\" and \"which caused\" to join a cause to its effect; instead, the reason and divide. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: students may talk through the change in the home language first; the teacher shows \"como resultado\" beside \"as a result\". Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-si-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue", "assumes": "g3-si-argue", "hands_on": "g5-si-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "I agree with; I see it differently; evidence; build on; decide; compromise", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)", "notes": "Students persuade their group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason, and reach a compromise, recording reasons on a claim chart. Entry points (all persuade the group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason and reach a compromise, recorded on the claim chart): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of options and evidence, a word bank and the cloze \"I think we should ___ because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"I see it differently because ___.\" and \"What if we ___?\", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, building on and summing up others' views to reach the compromise, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short persuasion with a claim, grouped reasons and a compromise; \"should\" for a proposal and \"I see it differently because\"; for example, therefore and compromise. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; evidencia and evidence, compromiso and compromise are cognates (compromiso can also mean a promise, which the teacher names). Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-la-narrate", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-la-narrate", "hands_on": "g5-la-narrate", "model_in": "story map", "model_out": "story map", "words": "narrator; describe; meanwhile; suddenly; as soon as; theme", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e4-theme-summary (reading literature, same text)", "notes": "Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's story: how describing noun groups and a time clause (\"As soon as the door opened\") slow a scene down. They retell the story with its theme, then build one scene with dialogue and description, rehearsing it aloud first, with the teacher and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all retell an ELA story with its theme and write one scene with dialogue and description): levels 1 and 2 with the story map, a word bank and the cloze \"When ___, ___ said, '___.'\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with time-clause frames (\"As soon as ___, ___.\") and dialogue frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, using description and pacing and saying why, the frame off by mid-unit after the first application day. Forms: a scene with an opening, dialogue and description; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and describing noun groups (\"the old wooden gate\"); meanwhile, suddenly and as soon as. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for describing words (enorme and enormous, misterioso and mysterious). Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 stories are longer, and theme and description are added; in grade 5, the owner year, the time-clause frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-la-inform", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-la-inform", "hands_on": "g5-la-inform", "model_in": "text map", "model_out": "text map", "words": "definition; a ___ is a ___ that; in addition; for instance; structure; subtopic", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-informative (informative writing); ela:e4-text-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e4-grammar (forms of English)", "notes": "Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text: how a definition sentence (\"A ___ is a ___ that ___\") and a signal word show its structure. They map the text's structure, then build an informative section grouped by subtopic with a defined term, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA text's structure and write a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term): levels 1 and 2 with the text map and a labeled diagram, a word bank and the cloze \"A ___ is a ___. It ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"A ___ is a ___ that ___. In addition, ___.\" and signal-word cards, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing a structure that fits the subtopic, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term; definition sentences and relative clauses (\"a tool that measures\"); in addition, for instance and definition. Home language: a student may group the facts in the home language first; definición and definition, estructura and structure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 text structure and defined terms are added; in grade 5 the definition cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-la-argue", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-si-argue; g3-la-inform", "hands_on": "g5-la-argue", "model_in": "text map; claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "opinion; reason; for example; therefore; in my opinion; should", "cross_subject": "ela:e4-opinion (opinion or argument writing); ela:e4-reasons-evidence (reading reasons and evidence)", "notes": "Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the author gives a reason and its evidence (\"For example, ___\"). They find the reasons and evidence, then build one paragraph of an opinion with grouped reasons, examples and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The full opinion piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all find an author's reasons and evidence and write an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the author's reasons, a word bank and the cloze \"I think ___ because ___. For example, ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"In my opinion, ___. One reason is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___.\", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, grouping two reasons with their evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 3 text map of grouped facts is faded here into the claim chart. Forms: an opinion paragraph with an opinion, grouped reasons and a conclusion; \"should\" and \"in my opinion\"; for example, therefore and one reason is. Home language: a student may plan the reasons in the home language first; opinión and opinion, evidencia and evidence are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 reasons and their evidence are the target; in grade 5 the opinion cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Builds on grade 3 SI Argue (talk with reasons) and grade 3 LA Inform (grouped facts), since ELD-LA has no Argue id before 4-5."}
{"strand": "ma", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-ma-explain", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-ma-explain", "hands_on": "g5-ma-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "place value; product; partial products; as a result; which gives; break apart", "cross_subject": "math:m4-multiply-multidigit (multiplication explained); math:m4-divide-multidigit (division explained)", "notes": "Students explain multi-digit multiplication and division from the math unit with area models and place value, step by step. Entry points (all explain multi-digit multiplication or division with an area model and place value, step by step): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled area model, a word bank and the cloze \"___ times ___ is ___. Then I add ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"First I break ___ into ___. As a result, ___, which gives ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5, the owner year, the area-model cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explained step by step so another person could follow it; \"as a result\" and \"which gives\"; place value, product and partial products. Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (producto and product, valor posicional and place value are close). Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 the steps are multi-digit; in grade 5 the area-model cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the MA Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."}
{"strand": "ma", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-ma-argue", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-ma-argue", "hands_on": "g5-ma-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "equivalent; justify; the model shows; therefore; I agree because; greater than", "cross_subject": "math:m4-equivalent-fractions (equivalence justified); math:m4-compare-fractions (comparison justified)", "notes": "Students justify that two fractions are equivalent or compare them with a model, and respond to a classmate's reasoning. Entry points (all justify that two fractions are equivalent, or compare them, with a model, and respond to a classmate's reasoning): levels 1 and 2 with fraction strips and the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze \"___ is equivalent to ___. The model shows ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"I claim ___. One reason is ___. Therefore, ___. I agree because ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution and saying which is stronger, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a justification with a claim, grouped reasons and a response to a partner; \"therefore\" and \"the model shows\"; equivalent, justify and greater than. Home language: a student may compare the fractions in the home language first; equivalente and equivalent are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on a visual model; in grade 5 the equivalence cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-sc-explain", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-sc-explain", "hands_on": "g5-sc-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "energy; transfer; as a result; which causes; erosion; model", "cross_subject": "science:s4-energy-transfer (host investigation); science:s4-weathering-erosion (host investigation)", "notes": "Students explain how energy moves from place to place and how weathering and erosion shape land, from models in the science unit. Entry points (all explain how energy moves from place to place and how weathering and erosion shape land, from the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model or data, a word bank and the cloze \"Energy moves from ___ to ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"When ___, energy ___. As a result, ___, which causes ___.\", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and linking it to the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the energy cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a cause-and-effect explanation through a system; \"as a result\" and \"which causes\", with one process noun (erosion); energy, transfer and model. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; energía and energy, erosión and erosion are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 explanations follow energy and change through a system; in grade 5 the energy cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the SC Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-sc-argue", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-sc-argue", "hands_on": "g5-sc-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "claim; evidence; reasoning; structure; works best; therefore", "cross_subject": "science:s4-structures-function (host investigation); science:s4-information-codes (comparing solutions)", "notes": "Students argue how an animal's structures help it survive and which way of sending a coded message works best, from the class's tests. Entry points (all argue how an animal's structures help it survive, or which way of sending a coded message works best, from the class's tests): levels 1 and 2 with the test results on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze \"___ works best because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Our claim is ___. The evidence is ___. This shows ___ because ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another group's claim with evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the \"works best\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with evidence and reasoning; \"should\", \"therefore\" and \"this shows\"; structure, works best and reasoning. Home language: a student may weigh the tests in the home language first; estructura and structure, código and code are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on gathered evidence; in grade 5 the \"works best\" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-ss-explain", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-ss-explain", "hands_on": "g5-ss-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "region; resource; led to; as a result; respond; which causes", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss4-regions-resources (host unit); social-studies:ss4-disasters-response (host unit)", "notes": "Students explain how a region's resources shape its life and how people respond to natural disasters, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how a region's resources shape its life and how people respond to a natural disaster, weighing which cause matters more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze \"One cause was ___. This led to ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Because of ___, ___. As a result, ___. ___ mattered more.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining causes with \"which causes\" and saying which mattered more, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5, the owner year, the cause-card cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with several causes linked to one effect; \"as a result\" and \"which causes\", with one process noun (the response); region, led to and respond. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; región and region, recurso and resource are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 explanations connect several causes in a region; in grade 5 the cause-card cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host's disaster unit touches disaster and loss, and some students lived through a hurricane, flood or earthquake; the family note previews it and offers the regions host for the talk task, and no student is asked about their own experience. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the SS Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one host unit asks for both."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "4", "skill_id": "g4-ss-argue", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g3-ss-argue", "hands_on": "g5-ss-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "primary source; the source shows; for example; therefore; claim; research", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss4-primary-source (host unit); social-studies:ss4-canada-mexico-research (host unit)", "notes": "Students make claims from primary sources and research in the social studies unit, citing the source. Entry points (all make a claim from primary sources and research, corroborating one source with another): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on the claim chart in pictures, a word bank and the cloze \"The source shows ___. Another source ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"My claim is ___. For example, the source says ___. Therefore, ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading of the source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the \"source shows\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from grouped evidence in sources; \"for example\" and \"therefore\"; primary source, research and claim. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; investigación and investigation are cognates; research shares no root with it, so the card carries both. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on one or two primary sources; in grade 5 the \"source shows\" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student from the country studied is never made the class expert or asked to translate; they choose their topic like everyone else."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-si-narrate", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate", "assumes": "g4-si-narrate", "hands_on": "g6-si-narrate", "model_in": "story map", "model_out": "story map", "words": "at first; over several days; the key moment was; by the end; our group", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e5-presenting (telling and describing aloud)", "notes": "Students recount a longer group project across several days for a new classmate, choosing the key moments and leaving out the rest. Entry points (all recount a group project across several days for a new classmate, choosing the key moments): levels 1 and 2 with a story map of the days and a time-word bank, the cloze \"On the first day ___. By the end ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"On the first day ___. Over the next days ___. The key moment was ___.\", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, cutting what the new classmate does not need, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount across days that slows down at the key moment; time clauses (when, as soon as) and the past progressive, with describing noun groups (\"the tall tower we built\"); over several days, by the end and at first. Home language: students may plan which moments to keep in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate momento and moment. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the tasks last several days, so choosing the key moments is the new step; the grade 4 picture notes are gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-si-inform", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform", "assumes": "g4-si-inform", "hands_on": "g6-si-inform", "model_in": "text map", "model_out": "text map", "words": "so far; we have finished; next we will; So you mean; summarize", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e5-presenting (telling and describing aloud)", "notes": "Students give a short report on a group's progress and summarize classmates' reports, checking they understood. Entry points (all give a short report on the group's progress and sum up a classmate's report, checking they understood): levels 1 and 2 with the group's progress on the text map, a word bank and the cloze \"We finished ___. Next we will ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"So far we have ___. Next we will ___. So you mean ___?\", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up a classmate's report in two sentences, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a progress report in two parts, done and next; present perfect for what is done (\"we have finished\") and \"will\" for next steps; so far, next and in addition. Home language: a student may draft the report in the home language and give it in English; progreso and progress are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the report is to the whole class and the listener sums it up; the grade 4 gestures are gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-si-explain", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain", "assumes": "g4-si-explain", "hands_on": "g6-si-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "so that; as a result; we decided to; process; why not", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)", "notes": "Students explain a group's process on a longer project and the reasons for its choices to another group. Entry points (all explain the group's process on a longer project and the reasons for its choices to another group): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of choices, labeled step cards, a word bank and the cloze \"We chose ___ because ___. As a result ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"We decided to ___ so that ___.\" and \"This caused ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, answering the other group's \"why not ___?\", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a process with the reason for each choice; \"so that\" for purpose and \"which causes\" for effect; as a result, process and choice. Home language: groups may plan the explanation in a shared home language first; proceso and process are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the explanation goes to another group, who ask \"why not?\", so reasons must answer another way; the grade 4 step cards are gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "si", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-si-argue", "standard": "~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue", "assumes": "g4-si-argue", "hands_on": "g6-si-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "should; I see it differently; the other plan; therefore; for example", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)", "notes": "Students make the case for a group plan to the class and answer a different plan with reasons. Entry points (all make the case for the group's plan to the class and answer a different plan with reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both plans, a word bank and the cloze \"Our plan is better because ___. For example, ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"We think ___ because ___. The other plan ___, but ___.\", off before the end-of-unit case; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the other plan's strongest reason, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a case for the class with a claim, grouped reasons and an answer to the other plan; \"should\" and \"the other plan ___, but\"; for example, therefore and better. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates plan and plan, and razón and reason as sharing a root but looking different. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the case is made to the whole class against another plan, not inside one group; the grade 4 \"What if\" frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-la-narrate", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-la-narrate", "hands_on": "g6-la-narrate", "model_in": "story map", "model_out": "story map", "words": "chapter; scene; while; was running; looking back; theme", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e5-whole-structure (reading literature, same text)", "notes": "Students take apart the passage where the ELA unit novel's chapters join: how the past progressive and a time clause carry the reader across. They map how the scenes fit together, then build a narrative scene with pacing and a reflective ending, with the teacher first and then alone. The full novel study and narrative stay in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA novel's scenes fit together and write a scene with pacing and a reflective ending): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of scenes, a word bank and the cloze \"While ___ was ___, ___. In the end, ___ learned ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with past-progressive frames and an ending frame (\"Looking back, ___.\"), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing which moment to slow down, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 time-clause frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene with pacing and a reflective ending; time clauses, describing noun groups and the past progressive (\"she was running when\"); while, looking back and chapter. Model: story map at the 4 to 5 stage, theme, scenes and pacing. Home language: a student may draft the ending in the home language first; the teacher names escena and scene as cognates, and capítulo and chapter as sharing a root but looking different. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: how a story's structure carries its theme, and writing with description, dialogue and pacing. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-la-inform", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-la-inform", "hands_on": "g6-la-inform", "model_in": "text map", "model_out": "text map", "words": "both texts; while; which; compare; for instance; precise", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-informative (informative writing); ela:e5-compare-structures (reading informational, same text); ela:e5-grammar (forms of English)", "notes": "Students take apart one paragraph from each of two ELA unit texts on one topic: how each signals its structure (compare or cause and effect) with relative clauses and linking words. They compare the structures, then build an informative paragraph with precise words in the structure that fits, with the teacher first and then alone. The full report stays in ELA. Entry points (all compare two ELA texts' structures and write an informative paragraph in a fitting structure): levels 1 and 2 with both texts on a text map, a word bank and the cloze \"Text 1 is about ___. Text 2 ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Both texts ___. Text 1 ___, while text 2 ___.\" and relative-clause frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing precise words for the topic, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 definition cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an informative paragraph in a structure that fits the topic; definition sentences and relative clauses (\"which\", \"that\"); in addition, for instance and while. Model: text map at the 4 to 5 stage, the text's structure (cause, compare, sequence). Home language: a student may compare the texts in the home language first; comparar and compare, causa and cause are cognates. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: comparing text structures and writing an organized piece with precise language."}
{"strand": "la", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-la-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-la-argue", "hands_on": "g6-la-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "this shows; therefore; the strongest reason; for example; should; point", "cross_subject": "ela:e5-opinion (opinion or argument writing); ela:e5-reasons-to-points (reading reasons and evidence)", "notes": "Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the author links a reason to its evidence with therefore and for example. They trace which reasons support which points, then build an opinion paragraph that links reasons to evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. The full opinion piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace which reasons support which points and write an opinion paragraph that links reasons to evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of points and reasons, a word bank and the cloze \"___ should ___ because ___. For example, ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"One reason is ___. This shows ___. Therefore, ___.\", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, ordering reasons from strongest to weakest, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 opinion cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons linked to evidence and a conclusion; \"should\" and \"this shows\"; for example, therefore and the strongest reason. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may plan the reasons in the home language first; the teacher names ejemplo and example, which share a root but look different. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: reading reasons and evidence, writing an opinion with linked reasons."}
{"strand": "ma", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-ma-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-ma-explain", "hands_on": "g6-ma-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "decimal; tenths; quotient; product; which means; as a result", "cross_subject": "math:m5-decimal-operations (decimal steps); math:m5-fraction-mult-problems (problem solutions)", "notes": "Students explain decimal operations and fraction word problems from the math unit, saying why each step fits the problem. Entry points (all explain decimal operations and fraction word problems, saying why each step fits the problem): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model (grid or tape diagram), a word bank and the cloze \"I multiply ___ by ___ because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"First ___. This step fits because ___, which means ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 area-model cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a problem's solution, step by step with why each fits; \"as a result\" and \"which means\", with one process noun (the product, the quotient); decimal, tenths and quotient. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes (more than one step can lead on). Home language: a student may talk through the problem in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates decimal and decimal, cociente and quotient, and that many countries write 3,5 where the United States writes 3.5. Owner year for MA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: following and writing explanations of procedures and problem solutions. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the MA Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."}
{"strand": "ma", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-ma-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-ma-argue", "hands_on": "g6-ma-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "larger; smaller; scaling; for example; therefore; should", "cross_subject": "math:m5-scaling (scaling claims); math:m5-shape-hierarchy (classifying shapes)", "notes": "Students argue whether multiplying by a fraction makes a number larger or smaller, and classify shapes in a hierarchy, defending each placement. Entry points (all argue whether multiplying by a fraction makes a number larger or smaller, and defend where a shape goes in a hierarchy): levels 1 and 2 with the model and the hierarchy chart, a word bank and the cloze \"It gets smaller because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"I claim ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___.\" and a response frame, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution and saying which is stronger, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with a claim, grouped reasons and an example; \"should\" and \"for example\"; larger, smaller and scaling. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may test examples in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for shape names (rectángulo and rectangle, cuadrado and square share a root but look different, so the card carries both). Owner year for MA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: making and critiquing claims with reasons from the work."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-sc-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-sc-explain", "hands_on": "g6-sc-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "energy; particle; matter; as a result; which causes; the model shows", "cross_subject": "science:s5-food-energy-sun (host investigation); science:s5-particles (host investigation)", "notes": "Students explain how energy in food came from the sun and that matter is made of particles too small to see, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain how energy in food came from the sun and that matter is made of particles too small to see): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze \"The energy came from ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"The sun ___. As a result, ___, which causes ___.\", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and linking it to evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 energy cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a system with a model; \"as a result\" and \"which causes\", with process nouns; particle, matter and the model shows. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; partícula and particle, materia and matter are cognates. Owner year for SC 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: explanations of systems with models. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the SC Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."}
{"strand": "sc", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-sc-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-sc-argue", "hands_on": "g6-sc-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "evidence; reasoning; gravity; supports; for example; should", "cross_subject": "science:s5-gravity-down (host investigation); science:s5-plant-matter (host investigation)", "notes": "Students argue that gravity pulls objects down and that plants get matter mainly from air and water, from evidence in the science unit. Entry points (all argue that gravity pulls objects down and that plants get matter mainly from air and water, from the unit's evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze \"Gravity pulls ___ because ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Our claim is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another claim with evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 \"works best\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with grouped evidence and reasoning; \"should\" and \"for example\"; gravity, supports and evidence. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; gravedad and gravity are cognates. Owner year for SC 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: claims with evidence and reasoning."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-ss-explain", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-ss-explain", "hands_on": "g6-ss-explain", "model_in": "cause chain", "model_out": "cause chain", "words": "cause; as a result; led to; tax; branches; which caused", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss5-revolution-causes (host unit); social-studies:ss5-constitution (host unit)", "notes": "Students explain the causes of the Revolution and how the Constitution organizes government, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain the causes of the Revolution and how the Constitution organizes government, weighing which cause mattered most): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze \"One cause was ___. It led to ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Because of ___, ___. As a result, ___, which caused ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several causes in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 cause-card cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of historical causes and a system; \"as a result\" and \"which caused\", with process nouns; tax, branch and led to. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; revolución and revolution, constitución and constitution are cognates. Owner year for SS 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: explanations of historical causes and systems. Teacher note: the host is about the causes of a war, and some students have lived through war or left one; the family note previews it and offers the Constitution host for the talk task, and the work stays on causes. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the SS Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one host unit asks for both."}
{"strand": "ss", "grade": "5", "skill_id": "g5-ss-argue", "standard": "WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Expressive", "assumes": "g4-ss-argue", "hands_on": "g6-ss-argue", "model_in": "claim chart", "model_out": "claim chart", "words": "corroborate; both sources; therefore; for example; civil rights; should", "cross_subject": "social-studies:ss5-sourcing-corroboration (host unit); social-studies:ss5-civil-rights-research (host unit)", "notes": "Students corroborate sources and argue a claim from research on a civil rights topic, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all corroborate sources and argue a claim from research on a civil rights topic): levels 1 and 2 with both sources on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze \"Both sources say ___. I think ___.\", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with \"Both sources show ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___ should ___.\", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading of the sources, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 \"source shows\" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from corroborated sources; \"should\", \"for example\" and \"therefore\"; corroborate, both sources and civil rights. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may compare the sources in the home language first; corroborar and corroborate are cognates. Owner year for SS 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: claims from corroborated sources. Teacher note: the civil rights topic comes from the host unit's list; no student is asked to speak for a group or to share family experience of discrimination."}



