{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-si-narrate', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate', 'assumes': 'g3-si-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g5-si-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'after that; meanwhile; finally; our group; at first', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud)', 'notes': 'Students recount a group task they shared (a project, a field study) for a classmate who was absent, in order, with linking words, then listen to a recount and ask a follow-up. Entry points (all recount a group task for a classmate who was absent, in order, then ask a follow-up of another recount): levels 1 and 2 with the group\'s picture notes on the story map, a word bank of time words and the cloze "At first we ___. After that ___. Finally ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "At first ___. Meanwhile ___. After that ___." and a follow-up card, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing what the absent classmate needs and leaving out the rest, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount for a listener who was not there, with an opening that says what the task was; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and the past progressive ("we were measuring"); after that, meanwhile and finally. Model: story map at the 4 to 5 stage, scenes and pacing (which moments get more time). Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language on the story map first; the absent classmate hears it in English. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.