{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-si-inform', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform', 'assumes': 'g3-si-inform', 'hands_on': 'g5-si-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'the task is; step; plan; role; Do you mean; clarify', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud)', 'notes': 'Students give and follow directions for a group task, report the group\'s plan to the class and ask for clarification ("Do you mean...?"). Entry points (all give and follow directions for a group task, report the group\'s plan to the class and ask "Do you mean ___?"): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a picture text map, gestures, a word bank and the cloze "Our plan is to ___. First ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Our plan is to ___. Each person will ___. Do you mean ___?", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up another group\'s plan to check it, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report that gives the goal first and then the steps and roles; "will" for the plan and relative clauses for roles ("the person who measures"); in addition and for instance. Model: text map at the 4 to 5 stage, the structure of the plan (goal, steps, roles). Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language and report in English; plan and plan, rol and role are cognates. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-si-explain', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain', 'assumes': 'g3-si-explain', 'hands_on': 'g5-si-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'because; as a result; so that; instead; the reason', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan, using cause and sequence linking words. Entry points (all explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of the change, labeled step cards, a word bank and the cloze "We ___. Then ___, so we changed to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "At first we ___. As a result, ___. So we ___ instead." off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, giving the reason for the change in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation that moves from the first plan to the change and its reason; "as a result" and "which caused" to join a cause to its effect; instead, the reason and divide. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: students may talk through the change in the home language first; the teacher shows "como resultado" beside "as a result". Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3.'}
{'strand': 'si', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-si-argue', 'standard': 'WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue', 'assumes': 'g3-si-argue', 'hands_on': 'g5-si-argue', 'model_in': 'claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'I agree with; I see it differently; evidence; build on; decide; compromise', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)', 'notes': 'Students persuade their group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason, and reach a compromise, recording reasons on a claim chart. Entry points (all persuade the group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason and reach a compromise, recorded on the claim chart): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of options and evidence, a word bank and the cloze "I think we should ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "I see it differently because ___." and "What if we ___?", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, building on and summing up others\' views to reach the compromise, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short persuasion with a claim, grouped reasons and a compromise; "should" for a proposal and "I see it differently because"; for example, therefore and compromise. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; evidencia and evidence, compromiso and compromise are cognates (compromiso can also mean a promise, which the teacher names). Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-la-narrate', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g3-la-narrate', 'hands_on': 'g5-la-narrate', 'model_in': 'story map', 'model_out': 'story map', 'words': 'narrator; describe; meanwhile; suddenly; as soon as; theme', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e4-theme-summary (reading literature, same text)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit\'s story: how describing noun groups and a time clause ("As soon as the door opened") slow a scene down. They retell the story with its theme, then build one scene with dialogue and description, rehearsing it aloud first, with the teacher and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all retell an ELA story with its theme and write one scene with dialogue and description): levels 1 and 2 with the story map, a word bank and the cloze "When ___, ___ said, \'___.\'", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with time-clause frames ("As soon as ___, ___.") and dialogue frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, using description and pacing and saying why, the frame off by mid-unit after the first application day. Forms: a scene with an opening, dialogue and description; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and describing noun groups ("the old wooden gate"); meanwhile, suddenly and as soon as. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for describing words (enorme and enormous, misterioso and mysterious). Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 stories are longer, and theme and description are added; in grade 5, the owner year, the time-clause frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-la-inform', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g3-la-inform', 'hands_on': 'g5-la-inform', 'model_in': 'text map', 'model_out': 'text map', 'words': 'definition; a ___ is a ___ that; in addition; for instance; structure; subtopic', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-informative (informative writing); ela:e4-text-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e4-grammar (forms of English)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text: how a definition sentence ("A ___ is a ___ that ___") and a signal word show its structure. They map the text\'s structure, then build an informative section grouped by subtopic with a defined term, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA text\'s structure and write a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term): levels 1 and 2 with the text map and a labeled diagram, a word bank and the cloze "A ___ is a ___. It ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "A ___ is a ___ that ___. In addition, ___." and signal-word cards, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing a structure that fits the subtopic, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term; definition sentences and relative clauses ("a tool that measures"); in addition, for instance and definition. Home language: a student may group the facts in the home language first; definición and definition, estructura and structure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 text structure and defined terms are added; in grade 5 the definition cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.'}
{'strand': 'la', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-la-argue', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g3-si-argue; g3-la-inform', 'hands_on': 'g5-la-argue', 'model_in': 'text map; claim chart', 'model_out': 'claim chart', 'words': 'opinion; reason; for example; therefore; in my opinion; should', 'cross_subject': 'ela:e4-opinion (opinion or argument writing); ela:e4-reasons-evidence (reading reasons and evidence)', 'notes': 'Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the author gives a reason and its evidence ("For example, ___"). They find the reasons and evidence, then build one paragraph of an opinion with grouped reasons, examples and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The full opinion piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all find an author\'s reasons and evidence and write an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the author\'s reasons, a word bank and the cloze "I think ___ because ___. For example, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In my opinion, ___. One reason is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, grouping two reasons with their evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 3 text map of grouped facts is faded here into the claim chart. Forms: an opinion paragraph with an opinion, grouped reasons and a conclusion; "should" and "in my opinion"; for example, therefore and one reason is. Home language: a student may plan the reasons in the home language first; opinión and opinion, evidencia and evidence are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 reasons and their evidence are the target; in grade 5 the opinion cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Builds on grade 3 SI Argue (talk with reasons) and grade 3 LA Inform (grouped facts), since ELD-LA has no Argue id before 4-5.'}
