{'strand': 'ss', 'grade': '4', 'skill_id': 'g4-ss-explain', 'standard': '~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Expressive', 'assumes': 'g3-ss-explain', 'hands_on': 'g5-ss-explain', 'model_in': 'cause chain', 'model_out': 'cause chain', 'words': 'region; resource; led to; as a result; respond; which causes', 'cross_subject': 'social-studies:ss4-regions-resources (host unit); social-studies:ss4-disasters-response (host unit)', 'notes': 'Students explain how a region\'s resources shape its life and how people respond to natural disasters, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how a region\'s resources shape its life and how people respond to a natural disaster, weighing which cause matters more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "One cause was ___. This led to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Because of ___, ___. As a result, ___. ___ mattered more.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining causes with "which causes" and saying which mattered more, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5, the owner year, the cause-card cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with several causes linked to one effect; "as a result" and "which causes", with one process noun (the response); region, led to and respond. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; región and region, recurso and resource are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 explanations connect several causes in a region; in grade 5 the cause-card cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host\'s disaster unit touches disaster and loss, and some students lived through a hurricane, flood or earthquake; the family note previews it and offers the regions host for the talk task, and no student is asked about their own experience. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the SS Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one host unit asks for both.'}
