157: 1 Anchors: three original Curio Class picture-book stories set today, each in a different place, of children who work to reach books and school (a girl whose library comes by boat; a boy who walks to a once-a-week book bus; a girl who learns to read beside her grandmother), 300 to 800 words, measure targets about 420 to 620; a short set of fables and folktales of learning from named cultures (300 to 600 words); an informational set on how books reach children (a paired original article on book carriers today and an anchor article on the 1930s packhorse librarians of Kentucky, built on public-domain federal records and photographs); a short Curio Class retelling of how Booker T. Washington learned to read, with a few lines from his own 1901 account, opened by one or two plain sentences on what slavery and freedom meant for him. Asking and answering questions with the place in the text named (RL.3.1, RI.3.1); recounting each story and stating its message with the details that carry it (RL.3.2, fables and folktales here, myths in unit 2); describing a character by traits tied to actions and how the actions drive events (RL.3.3); what illustrations add to mood and character, and placing each story on a world map from its pictures and words (RL.3.7, RI.3.7); main idea and key details of the informational set, of a text read aloud, and of a short recorded library presentation with a labeled bar graph of books lent each month (numbers within grade 2 math), told from the words, the picture and the numbers together (RI.3.2, SL.3.2); words and phrases as used in the stories, literal and nonliteral meaning told apart (RL.3.4, L.3.5a; real-life connections, 5b, and shades of meaning, 5c, continued on the unit 2 poems); a strategy chosen for each unknown word: context, a known affix, a known root, a glossary or dictionary (L.3.4a to d); the independent reading system launched (log, conference, book choice). Writing: the daily exit ticket (W.3.10) and an informative piece, one paragraph growing to two, on why getting books is hard in some places, with grouped facts, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.2a to d, W.3.4); a reading contract drafted, critiqued against one look-for and revised with peer and teacher feedback, and the reading-strategy bookmark planned and published (W.3.5: planning, revising and editing taught here; using technology to produce and publish, W.3.6, waits for keyboarding in unit 2). Fluency: a passage from a unit 1 story reread for accuracy, rate and expression and confirmed in context (RF.3.4a to c), then recorded as audio read for listeners (SL.3.5); poetry read aloud is added in unit 2. Talk: the discussion routine and norms (SL.3.1a to d), asking a speaker questions (SL.3.3), speaking in complete sentences when the discussion asks for detail (SL.3.6 in its grade 2 form; owned in unit 3). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.3.3a prefixes and derivational suffixes with meanings first; RF.3.3b Latin suffixes from unit 2, RF.3.3c multisyllable words from unit 3, RF.3.3d the year's irregular words all year); spelling on the same patterns, possessives (L.3.2d), suffix spelling and dictionary checks (L.3.2e to g); dialogue punctuation (2c) comes in unit 2, titles (2a) in unit 3, commas in addresses (2b) in unit 4. Retrieval: grade 2's wh-questions and simple and compound sentences; the fall oral reading screen is diagnostic (warm-ups). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.2a (unit 3); L.3.2b (unit 4); L.3.2c, L.3.5b, L.3.5c (unit 2).
158: 2 Anchors: original Curio Class frog poems and public-domain animal poems; two pourquoi tales from the base text (its own CC BY tales of why the frog has a long tongue and why the poison dart frog is so colorful, adapted) and short myths from named cultures; an original informational book on frogs (life cycle, adaptations, habitats, 400 to 900 words a section with headings, measure target about 600 to 820), and short federal wildlife pages on three unusual frogs (a glass frog, a horned frog, a water-holding frog). A frog's life cycle in order and why its skin or color helps it live, with time, sequence and cause words, and how sentences and paragraphs link (RI.3.3, RI.3.8); finding answers to the class's own frog questions with headings, sidebars, key words and an index, and on a teacher-chosen list of web pages (RI.3.5); general academic and domain words named as two kinds, with words of space and time (RI.3.4, L.3.6). Writing: a pourquoi tale ("why do tadpoles wiggle?") with a narrator, dialogue and time words, revised for word choice (W.3.3a to d, L.3.3a, with dialogue punctuation L.3.2c); expert-group research on one unusual frog, notes sorted under body, habitat and how it survives (W.3.7, W.3.8); an informative essay built to three linked paragraphs, planned, critiqued, typed and published as a page of a class frog book (W.3.2 and W.3.5 used again; keyboarding and publishing with technology begin here, W.3.6 from unit 1's e3-writing-process). Grammar taught on sentences from the frog texts and the students' drafts: regular and irregular verbs (L.3.1d), simple past, present and future tense (1e), comparative and superlative adjectives and adverbs (1g, "the smallest frog", "jumps farther"), coordinating and subordinating conjunctions (L.3.1h) and simple, compound and complex sentences combined from the frog texts (L.3.1i); plurals (1b) and abstract nouns (1c) are continued in unit 4, the function of parts of speech (1a) and agreement (1f) in unit 3. Review and extension: e3-literal-nonliteral on the poems and tales, now with real-life connections and shades of meaning for knowing and wondering (L.3.5b and c); e3-fluency on poems read aloud and a poem recording (RF.3.4b, SL.3.5); e3-word-strategies on the frog words; e3-main-idea and e3-central-message (myths here) on the tales and articles. Two frog texts read side by side without the comparison task (grade 2's comparison, in review; RI.3.9 is taught in unit 4). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.1a, L.3.1f (unit 3); L.3.3b (unit 3, unit 4).
159: 3 Anchors: book 1 of an original Curio Class chapter-book series set today (about 10,000 to 20,000 words, measure target about 620 to 820), read whole as a class; book 2 of the same series, about the same characters, read in part (at least three connected chapters, enough to compare theme, setting and plot) or as the independent book; a two-scene play script from book 1; a short informational anchor on how series books are made (g3-u3-04). Chapter, scene and stanza used when talking and writing, and how each part builds on the ones before (RL.3.5): what each chapter adds to the ones before, followed on the narrative arc; what the second scene of the script adds to the first; and, returning to one unit 2 poem of two or more stanzas, what the later stanza adds to the earlier. The reader's own view told apart from the narrator's and characters' (RL.3.6) and from an author's in a short article (RI.3.6); the two books compared on theme, setting and plot (RL.3.9). Writing: a book review recommending the series or not, with reasons in order, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.1a to d), taken through the process, with titles capitalized (L.3.2a); a scene from book 1 rewritten (review of e3-narrative). Speaking: the revised scene presented to the class as a report on the book with facts and descriptive details, at a pace listeners can follow (SL.3.4), in complete sentences where the task asks for detail (SL.3.6), read fluently (review of e3-fluency); the scene's spoken delivery set beside its written script, the conventions each uses named (sentences a listener can follow, a speaker's pauses and stress, against the script's punctuation, paragraphs and stage directions), then both rehearsed and edited to fit their own form, on a short paired model and a check where each student names and applies one difference (L.3.3b). Grammar continued from unit 2 on the books' sentences: what each part of speech does in a sentence (L.3.1a) and subject-verb and pronoun agreement (L.3.1f). A text-based discussion of the class's opinions. Review: e3-character-actions and e3-literal-nonliteral on the book. The unit's word list, studied and spelled.
160: 4 Anchors: an original Curio Class informational book on water on Earth (measure target about 700 to 820); two paired texts on the same topic, the anchor on how far people in one named region walk for water today and a paired text on how a US town cleans its water (federal water-agency pages, public domain); the base text's federal survey excerpt on population growth and water; an original story of a girl whose village gets a new well, read aloud. The most important points and key details of two texts compared in a table of points both make and points only one makes (RI.3.9). Three water issues (access, demand, pollution) researched from given sources (review of e3-short-research and e3-text-features); an opinion essay on why saving water matters, reasons from the texts (review of e3-opinion); a letter to a local water office, with commas in the address (L.3.2b; review of e3-informative). Grammar continued from unit 2 on the water texts: regular and irregular plurals (L.3.1b) and abstract nouns such as pollution and conservation (1c), with tenses (1e, the announcement's "we will") and comparatives (1g, "cleaner", "the most water") used again; parts of speech (1a) checked again. The year's spoken product: a water announcement (a public service message) on one issue, written, rehearsed and recorded as audio on a classroom device, then presented live with a reflection (review of e3-speaking-reports: SL.3.4, SL.3.6, and the script set beside its delivery, L.3.3b). Review: e3-text-connections (what happens when a well is dug), e3-point-of-view (what the author wants readers to believe about water use). The unit's word list, studied and spelled.

