157: 2 Anchors: an original Curio Class informational book on animal defenses (armor, poison, camouflage, mimicry; sections of 600 to 1,000 words with diagrams, measure target about 740 to 870, the lower half of the band); federal wildlife pages on a few defenses (public domain); expert-group texts on one animal each (a pufferfish, an armadillo, a skunk, a porcupine, an octopus); an original choose-your-own-adventure story as the narrative model. Main idea, how key details support it, and a written summary (RI.4.2); paraphrasing a short video or talk (SL.4.2); how a defense works, step by step and why (RI.4.3); chronology, comparison, cause and effect, problem and solution, sorted by structure (RI.4.5); a labeled diagram and a simple graph explained against the text (RI.4.7); two texts on one animal joined in writing (RI.4.9); the unit's words learned and used, with precise action words (RI.4.4, L.4.6), context clues of definition, example and restatement, Greek and Latin roots, dictionary and thesaurus (L.4.4a to c). Research in a notebook on the student's expert animal: aspects investigated, notes sorted into categories the student makes, a list of sources (W.4.7, W.4.8; the citation model introduced), drawing evidence from the informational texts (W.4.9b, continued from unit 1). Writing: an informative essay on how the animal's defenses help it survive, now with sections under headings, facts and quotations, linking words, domain words and a conclusion (W.4.2 used again on the essay-structure model from unit 1); a choose-your-own-adventure narrative about the animal, oriented, with dialogue punctuated (L.4.2b), description, transitions, sensory words and endings that follow (W.4.3a to e); both planned, critiqued, revised and edited (W.4.5 used again), and using a teacher-managed internet route with no student account, partners exchange a draft or comments, revise from the response and publish the finished writing, each student typing at least one page in one sitting (W.4.6, continued from unit 1). Grammar taught on the animal texts and drafts: modal auxiliaries (L.4.1c, "can", "may", "must") and adjective order (L.4.1d, "a small spiny fish"). Review: e4-evidence-inference, e4-discussion. The unit's word list

