172: 1 Anchors: an original Curio Class novel read whole (a present-day girl whose family loses its home after her father's death and starts over doing farm work in another state; chapters named for the crops they harvest; about 30,000 to 40,000 words, measure target about 750 to 950); articles of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in the official 1948 text, each with Curio Class's own short plain explainer (measure target about 850 to 1010); a short original two-scene play from the novel's world, the model for the monologue, and a fresh short scene read alone at the end of the unit. Quoting accurately when explaining and inferring, in talk and in writing (RL.5.1, RI.5.1, W.5.9a on the novel and play, W.5.9b on the declaration articles); a theme from how the girl responds to each challenge, and a summary (RL.5.2); two characters' responses to one event compared (RL.5.3); metaphors and similes interpreted in a passage, idioms and proverbs, synonyms, antonyms and homographs (RL.5.4, L.5.5a to c); how the chapters fit the novel's arc and the scenes fit the play (RL.5.5); stories, the play and poems read in the band, the fresh scene read alone (RL.5.10); two or more main ideas of a declaration article and an explainer, with a summary, and a summary of a short talk that shows a simple chart (numbers within grade 4 math), told from the words and the chart together (RI.5.2, SL.5.2); the unit's words, with contrast and addition words (however, although, moreover) (RI.5.4, L.5.6), context of cause and comparison, Greek and Latin roots, references for pronunciation and meaning (L.5.4a to c). Writing: daily exit tickets quoting the text (W.5.10) and a literary essay on how a character's response to a challenge shows a theme, connected to one declaration article: a general observation and focus, logical grouping, quotations, linking words (in contrast, especially), precise words, a conclusion (W.5.2a to e, W.5.4); a monologue in the voice of a character of the novel, oriented, with description, pacing, sensory detail and a conclusion (W.5.3a to e), built from short notes on the novel's setting and the farm work (W.5.8 previewed in its grade 4 form; taught in unit 2), planned, critiqued, rewritten where a new approach works better and edited (W.5.5), then performed and recorded as audio on a classroom device; the literary essay's revision typed (W.5.6 begins; two pages in a sitting and the shared-document exchange are continued in unit 2). Talk: discussions prepared, with roles, elaborating on others and drawing conclusions (SL.5.1a to d). Grammar from the novel's sentences: conjunctions, prepositions, interjections (L.5.1a), perfect tenses (L.5.1b), tense for time and sequence (L.5.1c), tense shifts (L.5.1d), correlative conjunctions (L.5.1e), expanding, combining and reducing sentences (L.5.3a; 3b, varieties of English, is taught in unit 2 with the narrators). Conventions in editing: series commas, introductory commas, yes and no, tag questions ("It's hard, isn't it?"), direct address, titles, spelling (L.5.2a to e). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.5.3a); fluency on the novel's passages and the monologue rehearsed for its recording (RF.5.4a to c). Retrieval: grade 4 fragments and run-ons and discussion roles; the fall oral reading check is a diagnostic. The unit's word list. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.5.3b (unit 2, unit 4).
173: 2 Anchors: an original Curio Class account of a canopy scientist's year in El Yunque National Forest in Puerto Rico (measure target about 850 to 1010); federal forest service pages on El Yunque, its coquí frogs, parrots and hurricanes (public domain); an original problem and solution article on protecting the forest; the base text's own CC BY texts on rainforest structure, adapted; two original Curio Class rainforest adventure stories in one genre (2,000 to 4,000 words each, measure target about 850 to 1010), one told in the first person by a girl lost for a day in El Yunque, one in the third person about a boy following a parrot survey, on the same theme of learning to read the forest. How plants, animals and people in the forest interact, from specific information (RI.5.3); the scientist's chronology compared with the problem and solution article (RI.5.5); answers found quickly across several print and digital sources, teacher-chosen first, then a supervised search (RI.5.7). How each narrator's point of view shapes how the same kind of event is described (RL.5.6); the two adventure stories compared on how they approach the theme (RL.5.9); a literary essay on how one story's sensory language builds its setting, quoting the text (W.5.2 and W.5.9 used again). Research on one way to protect the forest from several sources, summarized or paraphrased in notes, with a list of sources (W.5.7, W.5.8). Writing: a first-person narrative of a day in the canopy as a researcher or animal of the forest, with dialogue, pacing, transitional clauses and sensory detail (W.5.3 used again); planned, critiqued, rewritten and edited, typed at least two pages in a sitting, and partners exchange drafts and respond through a shared document on a teacher-managed route with no student account before publishing on a teacher-managed class page (W.5.6, continued from unit 1). Varieties of English named in the stories' dialogue and narration (L.5.3b; compared and contrasted in unit 4). Review: e5-several-main-ideas, e5-academic-words (biodiversity, canopy, species), e5-quote-accurately, e5-conventions in editing. The unit's word list

