135: 3 Life cycles watched first-hand (the fast-cycling plants or radishes sown on planting day, and mealworms becoming darkling beetles) and modeled as drawn cycles and sequenced cards; compared with other organisms' cycles (a butterfly, a frog, a flowering plant) to find that every cycle differs in stages and timing yet all share birth, growth, reproduction and death; the model revised when observation shows a missed stage. Traits: photos, tallies and bar graphs of puppies in a litter, seedlings from one packet and ear length in rabbits show offspring are like their parents and siblings vary. Then the environment, opened by one retrieval task on grade 2's light-and-water test: the class crowding test (the same seeds sown close together and spread out, the rest kept the same; crowded plants stay smaller) and other cases (the hydrangea whose soil decides its flower color, plants stunted by too little water, a dog fed too much and walked too little, flamingos fed less shrimp turning paler) support the explanation that the environment can change a trait; students sort traits into inherited, shaped by the environment, or both

