164: 6 A model that we see an object because light from a source bounces off it into the eye: drawn rays from lamp to object to eye, tested with mirrors, a flashlight in a box with a peephole and a dark room; the common idea that light goes out from the eye tested and revised, and the cat's eye shine explained as light from the flash reflected back, not light the eye makes; the dark box answers whether anything sees with no light at all. Senses: a flow model from sense organ to brain to action, tested with a dropped-ruler reaction game and other animals (an owl hearing prey, a snake sensing heat); animals use perceptions and memories to guide actions, shown with a new clap pattern that comes to mean a set action, counted in early and late rounds. Structures: an argument that internal and external structures support survival, growth, behavior and reproduction (roots, thorns, colored petals, heart, lungs, stomach, brain, skin), each part of a larger system; the plant half argued as fully as the animal half, opened by anchor B: external structures from a case set (a cactus's thick stem, spines and wide shallow roots; thorns; colored petals; the dandelion's seed parachutes); an internal plant case required, a celery stalk or white flower stem cut by an adult and stood in colored water, whose color rising through the tubes shows the inside structures that carry water from roots to leaves, roots, tubes and leaves argued as parts working together; a flower dissection (petals, stamens, pistil) required as the plant reproduction case; the unit's end task asks for one plant argument and one animal argument; the argument judged for whether its evidence is relevant and enough

