145: 2 An argument that plants get the materials to grow chiefly from air and water, not soil: the historical data (evidence against soil supplying most of the added matter; the experimenter concluded it came from water alone, a reading later evidence corrected), plants grown in water alone, and the class's own pots, set up in week 1 with dry-weighed soil, weighed again at the unit's end with the soil dried (compare plant gain and dry-soil change within measurement limits, with no promised result); then the air half: a supplied, sourced comparison of plants grown with the usual air and with more of the gas plants take in from air (as greenhouse growers add it), the plants with more of it growing heavier; students join the soil, water and air evidence and the particle model's air-is-matter in one argument; roots and leaves are the structures that take in the water and air; light is the energy, not the material. A model of matter moving among plants, animals, decomposers and the environment: the food web (who eats whom, with decomposers) and the matter cycle, including matter entering and leaving living things through air, water, food and waste, with a sealed jar ecosystem or compost bottle as first-hand evidence over weeks; students use the model to explain how several kinds of living things in one place each meet their needs, then revise an explanation for a supplied case of a new consumer with no predator (rabbits brought to Australia). Then models (diagrams, flow charts) showing that the energy in animals' food, used for growth, repair, motion and keeping warm, was once the sun's: sun to grass to cow to person, each arrow an energy transfer; energy flows through while matter cycles. Nature of science: one careful measurement overturned an old belief
147: 4 An argument that Earth's gravity pulls objects down, where down means toward the center of the round Earth: plumb lines and dropped objects everywhere fall toward the ground, and on a globe that is toward the middle; force arrows now point to Earth's center. Data in graphs revealing daily and seasonal patterns: shadow length and direction each hour, day length through the year, and stars or constellations seen only in some months; the Earth-sun-moon model as Earth spinning (day and night, moving shadows) and moving around the sun (different night-sky stars by season), checked with a globe and lamp. Then an argument that the sun looks far brighter than other stars because it is much closer: two equal flashlights at different distances in a dark hall; distances given as the time light takes to arrive (about 8 minutes from the sun, more than 4 years from the nearest other star, many more years from the bright stars of the night sky) and a scale model (if the sun were a grain of sand on the classroom table, the nearest star would be a grain of sand in a town far away), with no number over 1,000,000 and powers of ten only after math teaches them; light from a star travels to the eye and less of it arrives from farther away; the sun is a star

