248:| 1 | 3 | Getting to books. *What is the main idea, and how do the parts hold it up?* | 21 | g3-la-inform; g3-si-inform | WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform | ELA grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39), Reading against the odds: its informational set on how books reach children (the paired articles on book carriers today and the 1930s packhorse librarians), informative writing and main idea (e3-informative, e3-main-idea), class discussion (e3-discussion). ELD days 1 to 21, weeks 1 to 5. Inform | LA Inform in both modes (owner year; the K to 2 band's grade 2 began these ids): students take apart one section of the ELA unit's packhorse librarian article (how its heading groups facts under one main idea; "for example" and "also" adding a detail), map the main idea and details on the text map, then build an informative piece in grouped parts with headings, linking words and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The ids' parts, in our words: find what the text is mainly about and the details that hold it up; see how headings and grouping organize it; tell or write about a topic with a clear opening, grouped facts, linking words and a closing. SI Inform (owner year): share facts about the book carriers in a short sharing time, then ask and answer a clarifying question ("What do you mean by ___? Can you say more about ___?"). Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's paragraph with a topic sentence. Forms: text, an informative piece in grouped parts; sentence, a topic sentence and a detail joined with "for example"; word, main idea, detail, heading, grouped, and the topic words of the article. Model: the text map, 2 to 3 stage (grouped boxes with headings). The "tell, ask, retell" routine is taught in days 1 to 3 and kept all year. Shared task at three entry points: share what the class learned about one way books reach children and write a grouped piece about it (1 and 2 with a labeled diagram, the text map, the word bank and the cloze "The main idea is ___. One detail is ___."; 3 and 4 with heading frames and "For example, ___. Also, ___."; 5 and 6 with the text map only, adding a conclusion of their own). Change of audience: the sharing time told to a partner from the class, then written as a page for a grade 2 reader. Talk about language: what a heading does for a reader. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new short informational page on how a mobile library reaches a town (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and name its main idea and two details; share it with the teacher and answer one clarifying question; write a grouped piece with a heading; then a respond day. Out: the full research piece and the reading lessons (ELA). Tools: a model-sentence replay tool for the topic sentence (paper path: the teacher reads it). Curio Class writes the word cards, the frames per level, the model page and the new page | First because ELA's informational set runs weeks 1 to 8 and informing is the use the class needs for every later host. Grade 3 owns these ids, so the year opens on the one most English learners met last in grade 2's first unit. The talk routine and the 2 to 3 text map start here | Candidate: California 1.26, all four domains in the unit; PRIME Match.LE, one interpretive item on the new page; bar item 12, the self-check page's first use | Children kept from books by distance or poverty are the host's theme; no student is asked how many books they own or whether there is a library near home. Home language: a sharing turn may start in the home language; detalle and detail, idea and idea are cognates. Agent reads: sensitivity; spanish (family note) |
249:| 2 | 3 | Who decides in our town. *What does each level of government do, and why?* | 17 | g3-ss-explain | WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Expressive | Social studies grade 3 unit 2 (days 25 to 48), Who decides here: levels of government (ss3-levels-of-government); how the land changed (ss3-land-changes, social studies grade 3 unit 1, days 1 to 24) just studied and retrieved. ELD days 22 to 38, weeks 5 to 8. Explain; narrate (SI review) | SS Explain in both modes (owner year): students hear and read the social studies unit's page on who makes which rules (town, state, nation) and take apart one sentence that says what a level decides and why ("The town council decides ___, so ___"); explain what one level of government does and why, then how the town's land changed and what that changed for the people, with the teacher first and then alone, in a short paragraph. Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's resource-to-use chain. Forms: text, an explanation of a decision and its effect; sentence, cause with "so" and "because" and a present tense for what a level does; word, government, level, decides, council, land, local. Model: the cause chain, 2 to 3 stage. SI Narrate (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate, owned in this year's unit 8): recount how the town's land changed, in order, from the unit 1 map walk ("At the beginning ___. In the middle ___. In the end ___."); look-for: three parts in order with a time word. Shared task at three entry points: explain what one level of government does and why, and how the land changed (1 and 2 with picture cards of each level and of the land then and now, the word bank and the cloze "The ___ government ___."; 3 and 4 with "so" and "because" frames; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, one change in the land and its effect in a short paragraph). Change of purpose: the explanation given to a partner, then written as a caption for the class's town map. If later: days 22 to 24 come before the host's government pages, so they work the land change from unit 1, already studied. End-of-unit assessment (two days): hear a new short page on who fixes a town's park (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and order the chain; explain it to the teacher; write a short explanation; then a respond day. Out: the civics content itself (social studies). Curio Class writes the level and land cards, the frames and the new page | Second because social studies teaches the levels of government in weeks 5 to 10, and explaining what a level does follows informing about a topic. Narrate comes back early as SI review so it is not met only at the year's end | Candidate: PRIME Depth.KLU, the cause chain's links named openly; California 1.23, a long-term English learner note on "so" and "because" in writing | No student is asked how government works in another country or whether a family votes or is a citizen. Home language: gobierno and government share a root but look different; local and local look the same. Agent reads: sensitivity |
250:| 3 | 3 | The class deliberation. *What is our claim, and what does the source say?* | 19 | g3-ss-argue; g3-si-argue | WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Expressive; WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue | Social studies grade 3 unit 2 (days 25 to 48), Who decides here: its class deliberation on a local question and its vote (ss3-deliberation); a claim with evidence (ss3-claim-evidence, social studies grade 3 unit 1, days 1 to 24) retrieved; opinion with a reason (e3-opinion), ELA grade 3 unit 3 (days 81 to 119), later and not used. ELD days 39 to 57, weeks 8 to 12. Argue | SS Argue in both modes (owner year): students read two short sources on the host's local question and take apart the sentence that gives the claim and the one that gives its evidence ("The source says ___"); make a claim with evidence and take part in the class deliberation, with the teacher first and then in groups. SI Argue (owner year): negotiate a group choice: state a view with reasons, ask "Why do you think ___?", build on another's reason, and agree on a decision ("We decided ___"; "I changed my mind because ___"). Retrieval on day 2: grade 2's claim from sources and unit 1 of social studies' claim with evidence. Forms: text, a claim with two reasons and their source; sentence, "I think ___ because ___" with "the source says"; word, claim, evidence, source, deliberate, instead, build on. Model: the claim chart, 2 to 3 stage. Shared task at three entry points: make a claim with evidence and help the group decide (1 and 2 with the question and its sources in pictures on the claim chart, the word bank and the cloze "I think ___. The source says ___."; 3 and 4 with "My claim is ___. The source says ___. I agree with ___ because ___."; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two sources first). Change of audience: the claim said to a group, then written as a note to the town council. Days 49 to 57 run just after the host's vote, from the class's own record of it. End-of-unit asse