261:| 14 | 4 | The canal debate. *What is the author's reason, and what is mine?* | 17 | g4-la-argue | None | ELA grade 4 unit 3 (days 81 to 119), The Erie Canal debate: opinion writing and reasons and evidence (e4-opinion, e4-reasons-evidence). ELD days 93 to 109, weeks 19 to 22. Argue (SI review) | LA Argue in both modes (WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive and WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive, carried; owned in grade 5): students take apart one paragraph of the 1816 memorial or the unit's opposing speech where the author gives a reason and its evidence ("For example, ___"); find the reasons and evidence, then build one paragraph of an opinion with grouped reasons, examples and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. This is grade 4's first LA Argue: ELD-LA has no Argue id before 4-5. SI Argue (review; WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue, owned in unit 9): the group agrees which reason is strongest and reaches a compromise; look-for: "I see it differently because ___". Retrieval on day 2: grade 3's group decision (unit 3) and grouped facts (unit 1). Forms: text, an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons; sentence, "In my opinion, ___. One reason is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___."; word, opinion, reason, evidence, should, therefore. Model: the text map of the author's facts (model_in) grows into the claim chart. Shared task at three entry points: find the author's reasons and write an opinion paragraph (1 and 2 with the claim chart of the author's reasons, the word bank and the cloze "I think ___ because ___. For example, ___."; 3 and 4 with the opinion frames; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the other side). Change of audience: the opinion said to the group, then written as a letter to the 1816 legislature. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new short opinion on whether a town should build a bridge (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and find its reasons and evidence; argue to the teacher; write an opinion paragraph; then a respon